doc:sec-527r-ss-3 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "527R(3) Dealing with a sexual harassment dispute (other than by" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(3) If the FWC is satisfied that all reasonable attempts to resolve the dispute (other than by arbitration) have been, or are likely to be, unsuccessful, then: (a) the FWC must issue a certificate to that effect; and (b) if the FWC considers, taking into account all the materials before it, that arbitration under section 527S, or a sexual harassment court application, in relation to the dispute would not have a reasonable prospect of success, the FWC must advise the parties accordingly." ;
    cof:pageNumber "334"^^xsd:integer .

concept:fair-work-act-2009-sec-527s a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "527S Dealing with a sexual harassment dispute by arbitration"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-527s ;
    values:originalText "(1) This section applies if: (a) the FWC issues a certificate under paragraph 527R(3)(a) in relation to a dispute; and  Rights and responsibilities of employees, employers, organisations etc.  Chapter 3 Prohibiting sexual harassment in connection with work  Part 3-5A Dealing with sexual harassment disputes  Division 3 Section 527S Fair Work Act 2009 Compilation date: 02/04/2026 (b) 2 or more of the parties (the notifying parties) jointly notify the FWC that they agree to the FWC arbitrating the dispute; and (c) the notifying parties include at least one party that is: (i) an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute; or (ii) an industrial association that is entitled to represent the industrial interests of a person who is an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute; and (d) the notifying parties include at least one party that is a respondent in relation to the dispute; and (e) the notification: (i) is given to the FWC within 60 days after the day the certificate is issued, or within such period as the FWC allows on an application made during or after those 60 days; and (ii) complies with any requirements prescribed by the procedural rules. (2) The FWC must: (a) remove as a party to the dispute a party that is not one of the notifying parties; and (b) notify a person who is removed under paragraph (a) of the removal. (3) After doing so, the FWC may deal with the dispute by arbitration, including by: (a) making one or more of the following orders: (i) an order for the payment of compensation to an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute; (ii) an order for payment of an amount to an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute for remuneration lost; (iii) an order requiring a person to perform any reasonable act, or carry out any reasonable course of conduct, to redress loss or damage suffered by an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute; and (b) expressing one or more of the following opinions:  Chapter 3  Rights and responsibilities of employees, employers, organisations etc." ;
    skos:definition "(1) This section applies if: (a) the FWC issues a certificate under paragraph 527R(3)(a) in relation to a dispute; and  Rights and responsibilities of employees, employers, organisations etc.  Chapter 3 Prohibiting sexual harassment in connection with work  Part 3-5A Dealing with sexual harassment disputes  Division 3 Section 527S Fair Work Act 2009 Compilation date: 02/04/2026 (b) 2 or more of the parties (the notifying parties) jointly notify the FWC that they agree to the FWC arbitrating the dispute; and (c) the notifying parties include at least one party that is: (i) an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute; or (ii) an industrial association that is entitled to represent the industrial interests of a person who is an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute; and (d) the notifying parties include at least one party that is a respondent in relation to the dispute; and (e) the notification: (i) is given to the FWC within 60 days after the day the certificate is issued, or within such period as the FWC allows on an application made during or after those 60 days; and (ii) complies with any requirements prescribed by the procedural rules. (2) The FWC must: (a) remove as a party to the dispute a party that is not one of the notifying parties; and (b) notify a person who is removed under paragraph (a) of the removal. (3) After doing so, the FWC may deal with the dispute by arbitration, including by: (a) making one or more of the following orders: (i) an order for the payment of compensation to an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute; (ii) an order for payment of an amount to an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute for remuneration lost; (iii) an order requiring a person to perform any reasonable act, or carry out any reasonable course of conduct, to redress loss or damage suffered by an aggrieved person in relation to the dispute; and (b) expressing one or more of the following opinions:  Chapter 3  Rights and responsibilities of employees, emplo"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "4880567b9bc0f1c1809f4eb3b807ce4ac64647a5fda8758a0dcf6e1f60edc620" ;
    cof:pageNumber "334"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:fair-work-act-2009-sec-527s-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:fair-work-act-2009-sec-527s-ss-2 ;
